{"name":"Tropical Fruit","hex":"#fdd3a7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tropical fruit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any fruit that grows in tropical climates (between the Tropic of Cancer and Tropic of Capricorn), including bananas, coconuts, tamarind, and mangoes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tropical_fruit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tropical fruit","blocks":[{"text":"There are many fruits that typically grow in warm tropical climates or equatorial areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/ARS_tropical_fruit_no_labels.jpg/330px-ARS_tropical_fruit_no_labels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_fruit","wikidata":"Q844144"},{"title":"Tropics","blocks":[{"text":"The tropics are the region of Earth surrounding the equator, where the Sun may shine directly overhead. This contrasts with the temperate or polar regions of Earth, where the Sun can never be directly overhead. Because of Earth's axial tilt, the width of the tropics is twice the tilt. The tropics are also referred to as the tropical zone and the torrid zone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/World_map_indicating_tropics_and_subtropics.png/330px-World_map_indicating_tropics_and_subtropics.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropics","wikidata":"Q42530"},{"title":"Fruit","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a fruit is the seed-bearing structure in flowering plants (angiosperms) that is formed from the ovary after flowering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Fruit_Platter-_Seasonal_Fruits.jpg/330px-Fruit_Platter-_Seasonal_Fruits.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruit","wikidata":"Q3314483"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wik